const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Are melanocortin-receptor antibodies reliable?',
    a: 'Treat each one as unproven until shown otherwise. Across G-protein-coupled receptors — the family the melanocortin receptors belong to — a lack of selectivity has repeatedly turned out to be the rule rather than the exception, with many commercial antibodies binding tissue that no longer contains the receptor. No one has published the systematic knockout test for MC1R–MC5R antibodies specifically, so the reassurance simply isn’t on the record either way. The answer depends entirely on how a given antibody was validated, not on the receptor name on the vial.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a "knockout-validated" antibody mean?',
    a: 'It means the antibody was tested on tissue or cells in which the target gene has been deleted — and the signal disappeared. If an antibody still stains a knockout that no longer makes the receptor, it is detecting something else. A vanishing signal in the knockout is the single strongest piece of evidence that an antibody is specific, which is why it sits at the top of the validation ladder.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are GPCR antibodies so often non-specific?',
    a: 'Several reasons stack up: these receptors are expressed at low levels, they are embedded in the membrane with most of the protein buried, and the usable epitopes are short. For the melanocortin receptors there is an extra problem — the five of them are one close-knit subfamily, so an antibody raised against one can cross-react with its structural cousins.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I trust an MC4R or MC3R ELISA kit?',
    a: 'The same validation question applies to an ELISA as to an antibody for staining or a blot: what independent evidence shows it measures the intended receptor and nothing else? A kit that reports only a standard curve, with no genetic or orthogonal control, has shown you precision, not specificity. This page explains how to tell the difference; it does not endorse or rank any vendor’s product.',
  },
];

